            axiomatizes the properties of the Jordan triple product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{a,b,c\}
                  \\;=\; a b c + c b a
                  \\,.
          %p
            of self-adjoint complex matrices. However, this triple product also makes sense when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            are of a different type than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \times n
            matrices and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times m
            matrix, or vice versa. Thus, it turns out to be useful to generalize Jordan triple systems to
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Jordan pairs
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Loos77"} Loos77
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong Jordan pair
            is a pair of vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_-
            together with a pair of trilinear maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\cdot,\cdot,\cdot\}_+ \colon V_+ \times V_- \times V_+ \to V_+ \, ,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\cdot,\cdot,\cdot\}_- \colon V_- \times V_+ \times V_- \to V_- \, .
          %p obeying two symmetry axioms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi w
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\}_\pm = \{w,v,u\}_\pm
          %p and two Jacobi-like axioms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∓
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\big\{a,b,\{c,d,e\}_\pm \big\}_\pm - \big\{c,d,\{a,b,e\}_\pm \big\}_\pm
                  \\;=\;
                  \\big\{\{a,b,c\}_\pm ,d,e\big\}_\pm - \big\{c,\{b,a,d\}_\mp,e\big\}_\pm
                  \\,.
          %p This definition works well over any field or even commutative ring that is not of characteristic 2 or 3.
          %p The trilinear maps are often reformulated as quadratic maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_+ \colon V_+ \to \text{Hom}(V_-, V_+)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_- \colon V_- \to \text{Hom}(V_+, V_-) .
          %p
            For a simple example of a Jordan pair, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_+
            be a finite-dimensional vector space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_-
            the dual of that vector space, with the quadratic maps given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_+(v)(f) = f(v) \,v
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_-(f)(v) = f(v) \, f.
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V_+, f \in V_-
          %h2#advantages Advantages
          %p
            Jordan pairs are more general than Jordan algebras. Further, the connection between Jordan structures, 3-graded Lie algebras and bounded symmetric domains is arguably clearer in terms of Jordan pairs than using Jordan algebras or Jordan triple systems (
            %a{:href => "#Loos77"}> Loos77
            ).
          %p
            Isomorphisms of Jordan pairs are more general than isomorphisms of Jordan algebras or Jordan triple systems: they generalize the so-called “isotopies” of these. An
            %strong isotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon V \to V'
            of Jordan triple systems is a pair of bijective linear maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_+, f_- \colon V \to V'
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_+(\{u,v,w\}) = \{f_+(u), f_-(v), f^+(w) \}
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,v,w \in V
            %strong isomorphism
            from a Jordan pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\pm
            to a Jordan pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V'_\pm
            is a pair of bijective linear maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\pm \colon V_\pm \to V'_\pm
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∓
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\pm\bigl(\{u,v,w\}_\pm\bigr) = \{f_\pm(u), f_\mp(v), f_\pm(w)\}_\pm
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ±
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,w \in V_\pm, v \in V_\mp
          %p
            Unlike for Jordan algebras and Jordan triple systems, there is a natural way to define inner automorphisms of Jordan pairs (
            %a{:href => "#Loos75"}> Loos75
            ). (See below for inner derivations.)
          %p
            Furthermore, Jordan pairs always contain sufficiently many idempotents (which generalize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+operators"} projection operators
            familiar in the case of self-adjoint complex matrices) (
            %a{:href => "#Loos75"}> Loos75
            ). It may happen even in a finite-dimensional simple Jordan algebra that the unit element cannot be written as the sum of orthogonal division idempotents (the algebra need not have “capacity”). In a Jordan triple system the relevant generalization of an idempotent is a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong tripotent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p,p,p\} = p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            into a Jordan triple system with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mi c
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                  %mi b
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b,c\} = -(a b c + c b a)
            it has no nonzero tripotents. In the case of a Jordan pair
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_+, V_-)
            %strong idempotent
            to be a pair of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo −
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_+ \in V_+, p_- \in V_-
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∓
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo ±
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo ±
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p_\pm, p_\mp, p_\pm\}_\pm = p_{\pm} \, .
          %h2#relation_to_3graded_lie_algebras Relation to 3-graded Lie algebras
          %p
            A
            %strong 3-graded Lie algebra
            is a
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{g}_{-1} \oplus \mathfrak{g}_0\oplus \mathfrak{g}_1 \, .
          %p
            Given any 3-graded Lie algebra, the pair of vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{g}_{1}, \mathfrak{g}_{-1})
            becomes a Jordan pair with brackets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b,c\}_\pm = [[a,b],c]
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo ±
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,c \in \mathfrak{g}_{\pm 1}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∓
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in \mathfrak{g}_{\mp 1}
          %p
            Conversely, there is a functorial way to build a 3-graded Lie algebra from a Jordan pair. Start with a Jordan pair
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_+, V_-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{g}_{-1} \oplus \mathfrak{g}_0 \oplus \mathfrak{g}_1
            as follows. First, set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_1 = V_+, \qquad \mathfrak{g}_{-1} = V_-
          %p
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}_0
            consist of the
            %strong inner derivations
            of the Jordan pair. These are linear maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \colon V_+ \oplus V_- \to V_+ \oplus V_-
            that are linear combinations of those of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(x, y) = (\{x, y, \cdot\}_+, -\{y, x, \cdot\}_-)
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in V_+, y \in V_-
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(x,y) = (D_+(x,y), D_-(x,y))
          %p
            Next, define a Lie bracket on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            as follows:
          %p
            1)
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{g}_1, \mathfrak{g}_{-1}] \to \mathfrak{g}_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in V_+, y \in V_-
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x, y] = D(x, y) = (Q(x, y, \cdot), -Q(y, x, \cdot))
          %p
            2)
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mrow
                          %mo ±
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mrow
                          %mo ±
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{g}_0, \mathfrak{g}_{\pm 1}] \to \mathfrak{g}_{\pm 1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = (D_+, D_-) \in \mathfrak{g}_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D, x] = D_+(x) \; \text{ for } \; x \in V_+, \qquad [D, y] = D_-(y) \; \text{ for } \; y \in V_-
          %p
            3)
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{g}_0, \mathfrak{g}_0] \to \mathfrak{g}_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [(D_+, D_-), (E_+, E_-)] = ([D_+, E_+], [D_-, E_-]) \, .
          %p
            4)
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathfrak{g}_i, \mathfrak{g}_j] = 0
            when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |i + j| \gt 1
          %h2#relation_to_jordan_triple_systems Relation to Jordan triple systems
          %p
            There is a way to see a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+system"} Jordan triple system
            as a Jordan pair with extra structure, and vice versa (
            %a{:href => "#McCrimmon78"}> McCrimmon78
            ). This suggests that there are adjoint functors between the category of Jordan triple systems and the category of Jordan pairs.
          %h3#jordan_triple_systems_as_jordan_pairs_with_involutions Jordan triple systems as Jordan pairs with involutions
          %p
            Define a
            %strong Jordan pair with involution
            to be a Jordan pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_\pm, \{\cdot, \cdot, \cdot\}_\pm)
            with a vector space isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \colon V_+ \to V_-
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_-(\alpha(v)) = \alpha \circ Q_+(v) \alpha
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V_+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_\pm
            are the quadratic maps mentioned above.
          %p
            The category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+systems"} Jordan triple systems
            is equivalent to the category of Jordan pairs with involution (
            %a{:href => "#Loos75"}> Loos75
            ). In this equivalence, a Jordan pair with involution gives a Jordan triple system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \{\cdot,\cdot,\cdot\})
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = V_+
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\} = \{u,\alpha(v),w\}_+ \, .
          %p
            Conversely, a Jordan triple system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \{\cdot, \cdot, \cdot \})
            gives a Jordan pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V_\pm, \{\cdot, \cdot, \cdot\}_\pm)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_+ = V_- = V
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi all
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\}_+ = \{u,v,w\}  \qquad for \; all \; u,w \in V_+, v \in V_-
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo −
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi all
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\}_- = \{u,v,w\}  \qquad for \; all \; u,w \in V_-, v \in V_- \, .
          %p
            This has an involution given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = 1_V
          %h3#jordan_pairs_as_polarized_jordan_triple_systems Jordan pairs as polarized Jordan triple systems
          %p
            Define a
            %strong polarized Jordan triple system
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+triple+system"} Jordan triple system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \{\cdot, \cdot, \cdot\})
            together with a direct sum decomposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = V_+ \oplus V_-
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\}_\pm \in V_\pm
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,w \in V_\pm, v \in V_\mp
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ±
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{u,v,w\}_\pm = 0
          %p
            for all other possibilities, i.e. for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v \in V_\pm
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \in V_\mp
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v, w \in V_\pm
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in V_\mp
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v, w \in V_\pm
          %p
            The category of Jordan pairs is equivalent to the category of polarized Jordan triple systems (
            %a{:href => "#Loos75"}> Loos75
            ).
